From 981107ae1c1b214c74423a05bdb23b279bed0067 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 13:35:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] The trading post: a player market with bartering, and the
 name dialog it searches with

A player lists an item at a trading post and it leaves their inventory, held by the
post, with an optional buy-now price. Anyone can buy it now, or make an OFFER - coins,
items, or both - which is held too, so the seller's Accept completes the trade whether
or not the buyer is logged in, and nobody can offer money they do not have. Accepting
one offer hands every other offer on that listing back to its buyer. Whatever a player
is owed - sale proceeds, a declined offer, a barter's items - waits in their collection
box until they empty it at a post.

engine/market/TradingPost.ts IS THE MARKET, and it has no idea what a Player is: it
takes usernames, objs and a four-method "pocket" for an inventory, so every rule in it
runs under tools/tradingpostcheck.ts against the real class and a real in-memory
SQLite. That check ends every scenario by adding up every coin and item - pockets,
open listings, pending offers, collection boxes - and comparing with the start, so a
dupe or a void fails it whatever path produced it. It is in engine.yml.

ITS OWN DATABASE, AND SYNCHRONOUS. Player saves are files the login server writes, so
nothing can be given to an offline player through their save; the collection box is a
table instead. The rest of the engine's SQL is async Kysely in worker threads, and this
cannot be: "take the coins" and "record the offer" must happen in the same tick or a
second click spends the same coins twice. node:sqlite's DatabaseSync, a local file,
runs each action inline as one transaction, and every inventory move inside it pushes
its own inverse so a failed write puts the inventory back too. It lives at
data/tradingpost/<profile>.sqlite, apart from db.sqlite, so it works the same under
DB_BACKEND=mysql. It is one world's market.

World.autosavePlayer saves the player the moment a market action changes their
holdings, so a crash cannot roll an inventory back to before a trade the market has
already recorded.

THE SCRIPT SIDE is handlers/TradingPostOps.ts: thirty tp_* commands (7600-7699). A
window of listings is an inv the content transmits; tp_browse and friends write one
listing per slot and remember which is which for tp_slot. Every action returns '' or
the sentence to show the player.

P_NAMEDIALOG. The 377 client has always had the chatbox "Enter name:" prompt - ptype
6 out, opcode 206 back with the answer base37-packed - and the engine had the two
protocol numbers and nothing else. p_namedialog now opens it and last_string reads
the answer (a-z, 0-9, _; twelve at most), suspending exactly as p_countdialog does,
including being cancelled by the five places a new modal clears a waiting count
dialog. The compiler has no last_string pointer to set, so last_string is unguarded
and reads '' before an answer.

The one lint error and the one tsc error left are tools/invfullcheck.ts, and were
there before this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
